---
import Anchor from "#components/anchor.astro";
import Math from "#components/math.astro";
import Picture from "#components/picture.astro";
import Section from "#components/section.astro";
import Table from "#components/table.astro";
import { articles } from "#constants/articles";
import BlogArticle from "#layouts/blog-article.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import dxt1Result from "#assets/articles/dxt1-result.png";
import dxt3Result from "#assets/articles/dxt3-result.png";
import dxt5Result from "#assets/articles/dxt5-result.png";
import guildMarkIcons from "#assets/articles/guild-mark.png";
import pranDebug from "#assets/articles/pran-debug.png";
import tgaResult from "#assets/articles/tga-result.png";
import tgaRleResult from "#assets/articles/tga-rle-result.png";

const articleInfo = articles.find((article) => article.id === "aika-files-1")!;

const tableCols = ["Field Name", "Type", "Size in bits"];

const folders = `
. 
├── Ani 
├── bgm 
├── Effect/ 
│ └── STextures 
├── Env/ 
│ ├── LTile 
│ ├── MTile 
│ └── STile 
├── Mesh 
├── Objects 
├── Shader
├── Texture 
└── UI
`.trim();

const rle = `
while pixel_buffer.len() < (width_us * height_us) {
  let packet_header = reader.read_u8()?;
  let rl_packet = (packet_header & RLE_PACKET_TYPE_BIT) != 0;
  let packet_pixel_count = (packet_header & RLE_PACKET_COUNT_BITS) as usize + 1;

  if rl_packet {
    let color = read_bgra(&mut reader, pixel_depth)?;
    for _ in 0..packet_pixel_count {
      pixel_buffer.push(color);
    }
  } else {
    for _ in 0..packet_pixel_count {
      pixel_buffer.push(read_bgra(&mut reader, pixel_depth)?);
    }
  }
}
`.trim();

const rgb565 = `
let red = (color >> 11) & 31;
let green = (color >> 5) & 63;
let blue = color & 31;

let rgba = RGBA {
  r: (red << 3) | (red >> 2),
  g: (green << 2) | (green >> 4),
  b: (blue << 3) | (blue >> 2),
  a: 255,
};`.trim();

const dxt5alpha = `
let alpha0_weight = alpha0 as u16;
let alpha1_weight = alpha1 as u16;

if alpha0 > alpha1 {
  for i in 1..=6 {
    alpha_table[i as usize + 1] = (((7 - i) * alpha0_weight + i * alpha1_weight) / 7) as u8;
  }
} else {
  for i in 1..=4 {
    alpha_table[i as usize + 1] = (((5 - i) * alpha0_weight + i * alpha1_weight) / 5) as u8;
  }
  alpha_table[6] = 0;
  alpha_table[7] = 255;
}`.trim();

const dxt5alphaaccess = `
let alpha_indices = alpha_bits >> 16;

// Then, for each pixel:
let pixel_alpha_index = ((alpha_indices >> (i * 3)) & 7) as usize;
let alpha_table[alpha_bits] = 0;`.trim();
---

<BlogArticle title={articleInfo.title} description={articleInfo.description} date={articleInfo.date}>
  <Section hiddenHeading name="Introduction">
    <p>
      Some months ago, I bought a 3D printer with my girlfriend to print cool things that we find on the web. We started
      by printing gifts for our family and friends, including useful house items and characters from several universes.
      A friend of mine, Joseph, asked if it was possible to do something very specific.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is this game named AIKA Online, a Korean MMORPG that we played a lot in the 2010s. We met there after he
      inspected my character's profile and found it funny that I had Aldebaran, the Rifleman's mount, equipped on a
      Warrior. It was, indeed, comical, as the Warrior class is tall and muscular, and the mount was built for the small
      and thin Rifleman, creating a sort of an "elephant on a bicycle" situation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Joseph asked if it was possible to find 3D models for the characters of that game, to which I replied that there
      isn't much info about the game itself. Sure, AIKA had its popularity, but MMORPGs are already niche enough, and
      this game never had enough power to break out of the bubble. To make things a bit more complicated, there are
      tools like Bin Parser or Mesh Viewer, and I needed a more robust tool to visualize and extract the necessary files
      that my friend wanted.
    </p>
    <p>
      I then created a program that successfully extracted meshes and textures for a character. I started by reading the
      source code of two existing tools, Mesh Viewer and the JIT converter, and used it as a base to write my own
      version in Rust. However, both tools had problems: Mesh Viewer exported boneless meshes only as COLLADA files
      (with structural problems), and the JIT converter did not work with newer files that have different magic values.
      Because of this, I did my own research and experiments instead of reusing their code.
    </p>
    <p>
      Being frustrated by old tools for an old forgotten game was enough. So I created a new project that would allow me
      to open the game client folder and see each file with a click. Using Tauri, I made a simple interface that allowed
      me to see which files I could open, and what was going wrong with the parser.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every new file I could'nt open made me more curious about how the game handles those files. "Why are there so many
      image formats? Why are there file extensions with different letter casing?". What was once a simple project to get
      a mesh for a character, became a mission to understand what was going on and create the definitive tool.
    </p>
    <p>This article is being written to share my findings from this project, starting with the texture files.</p>
    <p>
      You may ask "why did you code everything by hand and didn't use lib x or y?", and the only answer I have is:
      because that's the fun part!
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section name="Client Files">
    <p>
      I will be using the client from the official Brazilian server, version 1156 - the latest version as of November
      16, 2026. Upon examining the game client's root folder, we can see that there is no need to decompile or use
      external tools to access the game's assets. Assets are directly accessible, organized in their own folders.
    </p>
    <Code lang="text" code={folders} />
    <p>
      What matter to us now is <code>STextures</code>, <code>Env</code>, <code>Texture</code> and <code>UI</code>. They
      contain image files with a <code>.jit</code> extension.
    </p>
    <p>
      We are going to look into audio files in the next chapter. Some files have convoluted structures or lack any
      patterns, which make them more difficult to parse. This is especially true for <code>.bin</code> files. Being able
      to open texture files will be a good start for us.
    </p>
    <Section name="Magic" heading={2}>
      <p>
        The first 4 bytes of each texture file contain a magic string that tells us which format the file is stored in.
        Luckily for us, there's no encryption on them, and each magic can be directly mapped to the following formats:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>JT20 => TGA</li>
        <li>JT31 and JT41 => DXT1</li>
        <li>JT33 => DXT3</li>
        <li>JT35 => DXT5</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I haven't found any info about why there are two magic strings that represent <code>DXT1</code>.
      </p>
    </Section>
  </Section>

  <Section name="TGA">
    <p>
      TGA is an image format developed by AT&T EPICenter (which later became Truevision) in 1984, being one of the first
      image formats that combined several capabilities like true color, transparency and lossless compression. Its
      simple binary structure made it suitable for video production and 3D rendering, contributing to its widespread
      adoption in game development.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is one of the easiest texture formats that we can read from AIKA's files, as most TGA files in the game are
      uncompressed. To start, there is a 22-byte header:
    </p>
    <Table
      columns={tableCols}
      data={[
        ["Magic", '"JT20"', 32],
        ["ID length", "uint", 8],
        ["Color Map Type", "uint", 8],
        ["Data Type", "uint", 8],
        ["Color Map First Entry Index", "uint", 16],
        ["Color Map Length", "uint", 16],
        ["Color Map Entry Size", "uint", 8],
        ["X origin", "uint", 16],
        ["Y origin", "uint", 16],
        ["Width", "uint", 16],
        ["Height", "uint", 16],
        ["Pixel Depth", "uint", 8],
        ["Descriptor", "uint", 8],
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      For our parser, we will ignore most of these values, using only <code>Data Type</code>, <code>Width</code>,
      <code>Height</code>, <code>Pixel Depth</code> and <code>Descriptor</code>. For each pixel, we read the following
      bytes:
    </p>
    <Table
      columns={tableCols}
      data={[
        ["Blue", "uint", 8],
        ["Green", "uint", 8],
        ["Red", "uint", 8],
        ["Alpha (if Pixel Depth == 32)", "uint", 8],
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      The
      <Anchor label="TGA specification" href="http://www.paulbourke.net/dataformats/tga/" highlight />
      has 9 different values for <code>Data Type</code>, but we don't need to parse every variant as all the game files
      contain a value of <code>2</code>, <code>3</code> or <code>10</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bits in the <code>Descriptor</code> contain more info about how the image should be built:
    </p>
    <Table
      columns={["Field", "Bits"]}
      data={[
        ["Alpha bits per pixel", "1-4"],
        ["Right to left", "5"],
        ["Top to bottom", "6"],
        ["Unused", "7-8"],
      ]}
    />

    <p>
      Remember that we are reading everything as Little Endian, and the <code>Descriptor</code> is no different. For
      example, if the image has only a right-to-left flag, the descriptor will be <code>00010000</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most files can be parsed at this point. However, I found that there are some textures that are missing some bytes
      at the end which can potentially crash the program. I tested a lot of files and it's usually a black or
      transparent pixel. In those cases, we can consider that the missing pixel channel is <code>0</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      See the result for a file named <code>disceroa.jit</code>:
    </p>
    <Picture
      src={tgaResult}
      alt="An image showing rock mountains with a red glow, buildings in the distance."
      caption="A loading screen texture, shown when visiting Disceroa."
    />
    <p>
      As you can see, we successfully converted our first textures without any issues. Sometimes, like in this case, the
      image does not fill the entire space, creating black or white bars. This is not a problem with our parser; they
      already exist in the original files.
    </p>

    <Section name="Run-Length Encoding" heading={2}>
      <p>
        There is only one texture file that I found that uses Run-Length Encoding (<code>Data Type == 10</code>), and
        because we are building a complete tool for our use case, we have to support it. This type of compression stores
        RGB color information in packets, which can be one of two types: Run-Length and Raw.
      </p>
      <p>
        A packet has a 1-byte header: the first bit identifies its type (<code>0</code> for Raw, <code>1</code> for
        Run-Length) and the remaining seven bits specify how many pixels are stored within, minus 1. This means that a
        packet always stores at least 1 pixel (when the count is 0) and can go up to 128 pixels (127 + 1).
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Run-Length: We read a single RGB color and push it into our buffer as many times as specified by the count.
        </li>
        <li>
          Raw: We read an RGB color for each pixel, repeating this process for the number of pixels specified by the
          count.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>The code looks like this:</p>
      <Code lang="rust" code={rle} />

      <p>
        Our buffer can be used in the same way as the uncompressed variant. See the result of
        <code>ItemIcons12.jit</code>:
      </p>
      <Picture src={tgaRleResult} alt="An image of item icons on a grid." caption="Textures for items." />
    </Section>
  </Section>

  <Section name="DXTC">
    <p>
      Originally known as S3 Texture Compression (S3TC), it is a compression format created in 1998 by S3 Graphics to be
      used in their GPUs, and later introduced in OpenGL and DirectX. AIKA was developed using DirectX 9, so we were
      going to find files with this compression sooner or later. There are 8 variations of the S3TC algorithm, the first
      five of which have the DXT1 through DXT5 codes assigned by Microsoft, and only three of them are used in the game.
    </p>
    <p>All DXTC files from the game have the same header structure:</p>
    <Table
      columns={tableCols}
      data={[
        ["Magic", `"JTxx"`, 32],
        ["Width", "uint", 32],
        ["Height", "uint", 32],
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      The payload contains pixel data stored in blocks, with their size varying depending on the compression. DXT1 has
      8-byte blocks, whereas DXT3 and DXT5 both support transparency by having 16-byte blocks. Dividing
      <code>Width</code> and <code>Height</code> by <code>4</code> gives us how many blocks there are on each row and
      column, respectively.
    </p>

    <Section name="JT31 and JT41 - DXT1" heading={2}>
      <p>Each block has the following structure:</p>
      <Table
        columns={tableCols}
        data={[
          ["Color0", `uint`, 16],
          ["Color1", "uint", 16],
          ["Color Indices", "uint", 32],
        ]}
      />
      <p>
        We first read <code>Color0</code> and <code>Color1</code> and parse them as RGBA. They are stored as RGB 565.
        For each color, we can parse each channel this way:
      </p>
      <Code code={rgb565} lang="rust" />
      <p>
        After parsing both colors, we generate a palette containing 3 to 4 RGBA colors. The quantity of colors in the
        palette depends on a comparison between <code>Color0</code> and <code>Color1</code> as unsigned integers, as
        follows:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          If <code>Color0 {">"} Color1</code>:
          <Math text={`Color2 = \\frac{2 * Color0 + Color1}{3}`} />
          <Math text={`Color3 = \\frac{Color0 + 2 * Color1}{3}`} />
        </li>
        <li>
          If <code>Color0 {"<="} Color1</code>:
          <Math text={`Color2 = \\frac{Color0 + Color1}{2}`} />
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        In the latter case, <code>Color3</code> is treated as transparent black (<code>RGBA(0, 0, 0, 0)</code>).
      </p>
      <p>
        Then we read the <code>Color Indices</code>. There are sixteen 2-bit numbers stored in each block, which map
        directly to indices in the palette, and the resulting pixels will have the colors pointed to by. Because DXT1
        does not support transparency, all pixels will have an alpha of <code>255</code>.
      </p>
      <Picture
        src={dxt1Result}
        alt="An image showing a sand texture."
        caption="tile05050.jit - A sandy ground texture."
      />
      <p>And there it is! We can now see the contents of every texture file that has a JT31 magic.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section name="JT33 - DXT3" heading={2}>
      <p>
        Although this variant supports transparency, it is a bit more simpler than the previous one. Each block has the
        following structure:
      </p>
      <Table
        columns={tableCols}
        data={[
          ["Alpha Block", "uint", 64],
          ["Color0", "uint", 16],
          ["Color1", "uint", 16],
          ["Color Indices", "uint", 32],
        ]}
      />
      <p>
        We start the same way as with DXT1 by reading <code>Color0</code> and <code>Color1</code> and parsing them as
        RGBA. This time, we don't need to compare whether <code>Color0 > Color1</code>, the palette will always have 4
        colors, and the calculation to get <code>Color2</code> and <code>Color3</code> is the same.
      </p>
      <p>
        What differs now is that we must read the alpha value contained in <code>Alpha Block</code>. For each pixel, we
        read 4 bits and multiply by 17 to get a 0-255 range.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here is the result of a file named <code>CH01010101.jit</code>:
      </p>
      <Picture
        src={dxt3Result}
        alt="An image showing half of a character's face."
        caption="The texture for one of the possible Warrior face options."
      />
    </Section>

    <Section name="JT35 - DXT5" heading={2}>
      <p>
        The block layout is exactly the same as DXT3, and the only difference is how transparency is calculated. DXT5
        stores the alpha block containing two 8-bit endpoint values and sixteen 3-bit indices, one for each pixel. Those
        indices select one of 8 possible alpha values that are interpolated between the endpoints.
      </p>
      <p>
        Similar to how DXT1 has two modes depending on whether <code>Color0 > Color1</code>, DXT5 has two modes for
        transparency depending on how the endpoints (<code>Alpha0</code> and <code>Alpha1</code>) compare:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          If <code>Alpha0 {">"} Alpha1</code>, the table will have 6 interpolated values;
        </li>
        <li>
          If <code>Alpha0 {"<="} Alpha1</code>, the table will have 4 interpolated values, in addition to two extremes:
          0 and 255.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>To find each interpolated value in the alpha table, we calculate it using a weighted average:</p>
      <Math text=`\\frac{((N - D) * Alpha0) + (D * Alpha1)}{N}` />
      <p>
        Where <Math inline text="N" /> is the number of steps that lie between <code>Alpha0</code> and
        <code>Alpha1</code>, and <Math inline text="D" /> is the distance from <code>Alpha0</code>. Note that
        <Math inline text="N" /> is the number of steps, not the quantity of intermediate values in the palette: if you
        cut a line 6 times, you will get 7 pieces. It should look something like this:
      </p>
      <Code lang="rust" code={dxt5alpha} />
      <p>
        To get a pixel's alpha value, we use the 3-bit indices stored after <code>Alpha0</code> and <code>Alpha1</code>
        in the alpha block, like this:
      </p>
      <Code lang="rust" code={dxt5alphaaccess} />
      <p>
        Now the program is able to parse all texture files from the game! See the result of
        <code>PranBaby033.jit</code>:
      </p>
      <Picture
        src={dxt5Result}
        alt="An image showing the face of a non-human child in different reactions and moods."
        caption="Textures of all reactions and moods of a child Pran with blueish hair."
      />
    </Section>

    <p>
      After successfully parsing all texture formats, I streamlined the app UI. When selecting the client folder, the
      program lists all subfolders and files, and I can open any supported file with just one click. The backend
      converts the image, sends a buffer to the frontend containing metadata such as size and compression, and the
      frontend immediately renders it. There's also a button to export the result.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section name="More findings">
    <p>
      Something that I noticed when looking through the files is that the developers of the game are both organized and
      chaotic at the same time. They thought about organizing the game's assets with some conventions, but apparently
      they are not so good in following them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most textures are grouped by conventions that relate to their use in the game. For example, all textures used by
      the player characters have the prefix <code>CH</code> (player meshes use the same pattern) followed by numbers
      that represent which character it is, which piece it is (face, chest, legs, etc.) and which armor set it belongs
      to. Weapons follow a similar convention, the prefix representing the class, weapon type and armor set.
    </p>
    <p>
      Not every file follows the same naming convention, though. Some files use CamelCase, others snake_case, and even
      kebab-case is used. In some cases, even files of the same family are outliers. For instance, all map textures used
      in the UI follow the pattern <code>Map[xx].jit</code> where <code>[xx]</code> is a number from 01 through 64. The
      first five textures have an uppercase "M", but <code>map06.jit</code> starts with a lowercase one. The rest of
      these files also use a lowercase "m", the only exception being <code>Map60.jit</code>.
    </p>
    <Section name="Guild Mark limits" heading={2}>
      <p>
        This is probably the oddest choice I've seen in this research. AIKA has a guild system with a somewhat low
        limit. My friends and I had a lot of trouble creating one, as the server was always full. I found files that
        contain all guild icons, going from <code>GuildMarkWorld0.jit</code> through <code> GuildMarkWorld5.jit</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each file was supposed to contain guild icons for a different subserver, but the Brazilian server never had more
        than two active subservers at the same time.
      </p>
      <p>
        One of these is named <code>GuildMarkWorld1.tga</code>, which is somewhat peculiar, as it is the only one to be
        stored as a plain TGA file. As far as I can tell, the game only uses this particular file, while the remaining
        ones have seemingly remained untouched for over a decade. Those leftovers should have been removed, especially
        considering that one of them contains some rather questionable examples of 20th-century German graphic design.
      </p>
      <Picture src={guildMarkIcons} alt="" caption="GuildMarkWorld1.tga - Guild icons as of November 17, 2026." />
      <p>
        It appears that the guild mark is hard-limited by how many icons can be stored in this file. All guilds from all
        nations are stored in it, which can theoretically store up to 676 different icons. The server, however, has a
        limit of 127 per nation, which results in 127 x 5 = 635 guild slots in the entire game.
      </p>
      <p>
        This means that, even if the server doesn't have a limit, the client will not be able to handle a large number
        of guilds. The guild mark system would have had to be rewritten, allowing each guild to have its own unique
        texture file stored on the server rather than in the client.
      </p>
    </Section>
    <Section name="Debug/Beta files" heading={2}>
      <p>
        Probably the funniest part of looking into a game's assets is finding things that the player should not see. I
        never thought I would find assets like this, as the game has been running for more than a decade and they should
        have been removed.
      </p>
      <p>To start, I found files that are completely empty:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <code>z011410.jit</code> and <code>z011411.jit</code> are both a 4×4 black square;
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>z011413.jit</code> and <code>z011414.jit</code> are both a 4×4 gray square;
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>sz010812.jit</code> and <code>sz010813.jit</code> are both a 4×4 white square;
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>black256.jit</code> is a 256×256 black square;
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>blank.jit</code> is a 128×128 black square;
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>Blank.jit</code> (with uppercase M) is a 256×256 image that is completly transparent.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>And the most interesting file I found is:</p>
      <Picture alt="Pran01.jit" src={pranDebug} caption="Pran01.jit - A beta texture for the Pran states." />
    </Section>
  </Section>

  <Section name="Final thoughts" heading={2}>
    <p>
      This has been a great but challenging experience. I've had a lot of fun analyzing files from a game I loved in my
      childhood.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'm not done yet; there's still a lot of ground to cover. In the next chapter, we are going to see audio files.
    </p>
    <p>The tool will be released soon, once it has enough features and they are working properly.</p>
    <p>Thank you for reading!</p>
  </Section>
</BlogArticle>
